.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt RESIZE 1
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm resize
.Nd ask the terminal how big it is, and tell the kernel
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A tty has a window size and every full-screen program reads it: ncurses sizes
its screen from it, readline decides where a line wraps by it.
On the video console the kernel knows the answer, because it is drawing the
characters.
On a serial line nobody does.
There is no
.Dv SIGWINCH
over a serial cable and no in-band way to announce a resize, so
.Xr agetty 8
stamps the tty with 24 rows by 80 columns and that number stays there for the
life of the session no matter what is really at the other end.
.Pp
Being wrong about the width is worse than it sounds.
Bash believes the line ends at column 80, the terminal wraps it at 140, and
from then on every redraw of the prompt is computed from a cursor position that
does not exist: output lands on top of itself and the session looks stuck on
one line.
.Ic clear
appearing to fix it is the symptom, not a fix.
.Pp
.Nm
asks the terminal instead.
It parks the cursor far past any real corner - the terminal clamps the move to
its own last row and column - and then sends the Device Status Report for the
cursor position,
.Ql ESC [ 6 n ,
which is answered with
.Ql ESC [ <row> ; <col> R .
That answer is the size.
It sets it on the tty with
.Xr stty 1
and prints it as shell assignments, so that
.Ic eval $(resize)
sets
.Ev COLUMNS
and
.Ev LINES
in the shell as well.
This is what xterm's
.Nm
has always done, and over a line carrying nothing but bytes it is the only
method there is.
.Pp
It takes no options.
The query, the reply and the
.Xr stty 1
calls all go to
.Pa /dev/tty
rather than to standard output, which is what makes
.Ic eval $(resize)
work: with output going down a pipe, a query printed there would never reach
the terminal and no reply would ever come.
.Pp
Sowa runs it at login from
.Pa /etc/profile.d/winsize.sh ,
so a serial session is the right size from the first prompt.
Run it by hand after resizing the window at the other end.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/profile.d/winsize.sh" -compact
.It Pa /dev/tty
the terminal, asked directly.
.It Pa /etc/profile.d/winsize.sh
what runs this at login.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 0 -compact
.It 0
the terminal answered and the size was set.
.It 1
it did not answer within the second it is given.
.It 2
there is no controlling terminal.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Set the tty's size, and the shell's idea of it:
.Pp
.Dl eval $(resize)
.Pp
Just the numbers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ resize
COLUMNS=140;
LINES=44;
export COLUMNS LINES;
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr stty 1 ,
.Xr agetty 8 ,
.Xr serial-getty 8
.Sh CAVEATS
A terminal that does not answer within a second is left alone, which covers a
real terminal too old to reply and a pipe that is not a terminal at all.
Nothing is changed in that case, and the old size stands.
